  "slug": "measuring-an-algorithm",
  "title": "How an algorithm is measured",
  "shortTitle": "Measurement",
  "order": 1,
  "locale": "en",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "status": "published",
  "summary": "What efficiency means in algorithm design and analysis, how operations are counted, and how cost grows with input size. AALIE provides observable counting and basic comparisons without replacing your reasoning.",
  "difficulty": "foundational",
  "estimatedMinutes": 35,
  "tags": ["efficiency", "operations", "n", "counting", "comparison"],
  "searchMeta": {
    "aliases": ["measure algorithm", "efficiency"],
    "keywords": ["big O", "elementary operation", "growth", "n"]
  },
  "prerequisites": { "modules": [], "sections": [] },
  "relatedModuleIds": ["mod-user-guide-building-cost"],
  "learningObjectives": [
    {
      "objectiveId": "obj-eficiencia",
      "text": "Explain efficiency as growth of cost with input size, not raw speed on a particular machine."
    },
    {
      "objectiveId": "obj-operaciones",
      "text": "Identify elementary operations in pseudocode and relate them to what line counting shows."
    }
  ],
  "terms": [
    {
      "termId": "term-tamano-entrada",
      "label": "input size",
      "aliases": ["n", "size"],
      "definition": "The measure of the problem used as the variable in the analysis (e.g. array length), often denoted n."
    }
  ],
  "chapters": [
    {
      "chapterId": "cap-medir-fundamentos",
      "slug": "fundamentals",
      "title": "Fundamentals",
      "order": 1,
      "summary": "From the idea of efficiency to counting operations and growth in n.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "sectionId": "sec-que-es-eficiencia",
          "slug": "what-efficiency-is",
          "title": "What efficiency is (and is not)",
          "order": 1,
          "kind": "theory",
          "trackProgress": true,
          "estimatedMinutes": 12,
          "searchMeta": {
            "keywords": ["efficiency", "hardware", "time"]
          },
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "blk-m1-s1-intro",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "In algorithm design and analysis, efficiency describes the "
                },
                {
                  "type": "strong",
                  "text": "growth of cost"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " as the input size grows. It is not a race on a specific computer: the same algorithm may run faster or slower on different hardware, but the "
                },
                {
                  "type": "strong",
                  "text": "order of growth"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " with respect to n is a property of the method you study."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "blk-m1-s1-evidencia",
              "type": "evidenceBlock",
              "variant": "systemEvidence",
              "icon": "analytics",
              "title": "What evidence AALIE adds here",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "id": "blk-m1-s1-ev-p1",
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Per-line counting and basic comparisons let you check that the operation model matches what the program does. If the work per line does not depend on n but the loop runs n times, the total cost cannot be constant: that evidence supports a linear-growth conclusion."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "sectionId": "sec-operaciones-y-n",
          "slug": "operations-and-n",
          "title": "Operations and growth in n",
          "order": 2,
          "kind": "theory",
          "trackProgress": true,
          "estimatedMinutes": 14,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "blk-m1-s2-p1",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "In the usual RAM model, assignments, basic arithmetic, comparisons, and indexed memory accesses are treated as constant cost. Analysis counts how often they run as a function of n, then sums. You do not need internal names: what matters is that counting lets you "
                },
                {
                  "type": "strong",
                  "text": "justify"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " how many times each part of the code runs."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "blk-m1-s2-p2",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "You write pseudocode in the editor, validate that syntax is accepted, then analyze and interpret. You do not need the technical pipeline: it is enough to know that results reflect counting over executing lines."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "blk-m1-s2-example",
              "type": "evidenceBlock",
              "variant": "example",
              "icon": "science",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "id": "blk-m1-s2-p3",
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "If a traversal visits each position of an array of length n once, traversal cost grows linearly with n. Comparing with another fragment that does the same lets you check both models agree before claiming equivalent order."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "sectionId": "sec-sintaxis-minima",
          "slug": "minimal-syntax",
          "title": "Minimal syntax in the examples",
          "order": 3,
          "kind": "reference",
          "trackProgress": true,
          "estimatedMinutes": 9,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "blk-m1-s3-p1",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Examples use pseudocode with assignment "
                },
                { "type": "inlineCode", "text": "<-" },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ", "
                },
                { "type": "inlineCode", "text": "FOR" },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " and "
                },
                { "type": "inlineCode", "text": "WHILE" },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " loops, "
                },
                { "type": "inlineCode", "text": "IF" },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " conditionals, and "
                },
                { "type": "inlineCode", "text": "CALL" },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " when needed. There is no separate grammar chapter: the visible form is what the analyzer accepts; fix validation issues before interpreting results."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "blk-m1-s3-note",
              "type": "note",
              "variant": "info",
              "title": "Note",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "id": "blk-m1-s3-note-p",
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Syntax details do not replace analysis: they only enable meaningful counting and tracing on the code you write."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
